-- title:
--   j(mathsf q^ℓ) lies in the level-(qℓ)²M' field and is integral over A[j(mathsf q)]
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ and $\ell$ be primes, let $M'$ be a non-zero natural number, and let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero. Let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((\mathsf q))$ (Laurent series over $L$) assumed equal to [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) applied to [`ModularCurve.xHFunctionField ((q*ℓ)^2*M') (ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH (q*ℓ) M')`](def/ModularCurve_XH.html#L79), that is: $K$ is the subfield of $L((\mathsf q))$ generated over $L$ by the image, under the coefficientwise map $\mathbb Q((\mathsf q)) \to L((\mathsf q))$ induced by $\mathbb Q \to L$, of the $\mathsf q$-expansion function field over $\mathbb Q$ of level $\Gamma_H$ with $H \le (\mathbb Z/(q\ell)^2M')^{\times}$ the kernel of the reduction map to $(\mathbb Z/q\ell)^{\times}$. Let $A$ be a commutative ring with algebra structures on $L$ and on $K$ forming a scalar tower. Then there are proofs that: the image $j(\mathsf q)$ of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) (the Laurent series $\mathsf q^{-1}$ times the rational power series `jNumQ`) under the coefficientwise map lies in $K$; the resulting element of $K$ is non-zero; and its dilate under [`ModularCurve.qExpand L ℓ`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25) (substitution $\mathsf q \mapsto \mathsf q^{\ell}$ on exponents), namely $j(\mathsf q^{\ell})$, also lies in $K$; and moreover $j(\mathsf q^{\ell})$, as an element of $K$, lies in [`AlgebraicCurve.TwoChartIntegralModel.chartAlgFin A K ⟨_, hj⟩`](def/AlgebraicCurve_TwoChartIntegralModel.html#L142), i.e. it is integral over the $A$-subalgebra $A[j(\mathsf q)]$ of $K$ generated by $j(\mathsf q)$.
--
--   This is the input, on the $\mathsf q$-expansion side, for the finite chart of the two-chart integral model of the modular curve: the classical facts that $j(\mathsf q^{\ell})$ is a modular function for the rigid level $\Gamma_H((q\ell)^2M')$ and that the pair $(j(\mathsf q), j(\mathsf q^{\ell}))$ satisfies the modular equation $\Phi_{\ell}$, monic in its second variable. It is used by the constructions of rigid and Drinfeld charts at full level, in particular in the analysis of stalks and inertia at the primes above $q$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_FullLevel_qExpand_coeffEmb_jq_mem_and_mem_chartAlgFin_laurentBaseChange_xHFunctionField.lean

